layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | Buildyboy sees a lot: your prompts, Claude's replies, the commands it runs and the code it changes. This page
    | explains what is stored, how it is protected, who can do what, and what you can do to tighten it further.

  h2#stored What is stored, and where
  p
    | Everything is stored in one database on
    strong your
    | server; nothing is sent to anyone else. Notifications go through your browser's push service (Google, Apple or
    | Mozilla) or ntfy if you set it up, and carry only a short title, the project and the session name.
  ul
    li
      strong Stored:
      | session events, your prompts and Claude's messages, tool calls with their input and output (long outputs are
      | shortened), file diffs, git state, PROGRESS.md notes, saved prompts, job output, settings.
    li
      strong Never stored as plain text:
      | your password (stored as a scrypt hash), machine keys (stored as hashes; the machine keeps the only copy).
    li
      strong Kept for a limited time:
      | raw hook events and job output are deleted after the number of days in
      a(href="/docs/settings#budgets") Keep raw events
      | (90 by default).

  h2#redaction Secrets are masked first
  p
    | Before anything is written to the database, Buildyboy replaces secrets with
    code [REDACTED]
    | : API keys and tokens of the common services, JWTs, Bearer headers, password and token values, private keys, and
    | the entire content of any
    code .env
    | file Claude reads or writes. You can add rules of your own; see
    a(href="/docs/settings#redaction") Redaction rules
    | . Masking happens on the way in, so it also applies to imported history.
  +callout(variant="muted")
    @content
      p
        | Masking is pattern-based. A secret in an unusual format can slip through. If you work with such secrets, add a
        | rule for them, and avoid pasting secrets into prompts.

  h2#web-access Who can use the web app
  ul
    li There is one password. Anyone with it has full access.
    li
      | Logins last 30 days and are kept in a cookie that scripts cannot read and that other sites cannot use (HttpOnly,
      | SameSite=Strict, and Secure over https). Every change also requires a second, per-login token, which blocks
      | cross-site request forgery.
    li After 10 wrong passwords from one address within a minute, logins from that address are refused for a minute.
    li
      | Settings → Devices shows every logged-in browser and lets you log any of them out. Changing the password logs out
      | all the others.
    li This manual is public on purpose: it contains no data.

  h2#machine-access What a machine can do
  ul
    li Each machine has its own key, created through a single-use pairing code that expires after 10 minutes.
    li A machine can only send events about its own sessions and answer its own sessions' requests. It cannot read anything else.
    li
      | A leaked key can be replaced (
      strong Rotate token
      | ) or cut off for good (
      strong Revoke
      | ) on the Machines page.

  h2#what-runs What runs where
  ul
    li
      strong The server never runs Claude or any command.
      | It records, displays and relays answers.
    li
      strong The plugin
      | runs a short Node.js script for each hook. It fails open: if anything goes wrong it does nothing and lets Claude
      | Code continue normally. With walk mode off it never changes what Claude does.
    li
      strong The runner
      | only starts
      code claude
      | with a checked list of options (no shell), only inside the allowed roots listed in its own settings file, which
      | the server cannot change. The only other commands it runs are a job's own Done when commands, and only after you
      | confirm them on the job page.

  h2#permissions Choosing permission modes for jobs
  p
    | The most important safety decision is yours: the permission mode of each job.
    strong bypassPermissions
    | lets Claude run anything without asking; use it only in a worktree or a disposable folder.
    strong acceptEdits
    | with walk mode on is a good everyday choice: Claude edits freely but asks you before running commands. See
    a(href="/docs/jobs#permission-modes") Choosing a permission mode
    | .

  h2#hardening Tightening things up
  ol
    li Use a long, unique password, and log out devices you no longer use.
    li Keep the server listening on 127.0.0.1 behind Caddy (the default), so it is only reachable over https.
    li Keep the allowed roots in each runner.json as narrow as possible, for example one folder of repositories rather than your whole home folder.
    li Revoke machines you no longer use.
    li Add redaction rules for any internal secret formats you use.
    li Keep your ntfy topic name long and random, or do not use ntfy.
    li
      | Copy backups off the server regularly; they contain everything above. See
      a(href="/docs/server#backups") Backups
      | .
